|~ `::doc`, the kind of a page, and what is declared under it: its
   constructor and the refusals of a payload that is no vector of
   segments, and the verbs that read a doc.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:doc
  |~~ A doc is prose held as a value, text that carries quotes of qlang:
      the page of every name. `doc` answers the page of a name,
      `::vec | doc`, and since a page is a value, its code is checked
      and the next step takes it apart.

      It is written between bars and tildes, a block or a line, markdown
      in essence: text with code in backquotes and quotes of qlang among
      it, ::snippet~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | segments) showing the parts
      of one. A quote under `::link` is a link to another page, which
      `open` follows, one under `::snippet` a query a reader runs to see
      what it shows, and a quote with no tag code the prose holds. JSON
      writes a doc as its print.

      `content` answers its text, `segments` its prose and its quotes in
      order, `links` its links and `snippets` its snippets, so
      `::qlang | doc | links | map ~open` opens every page the first
      page names.
      ::snippet~(::doc | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::link | doc) ::link~(::snippet | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps
        |~~ A vector of prose strings and quotes, built into the doc. ~~|
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::doc` builds a doc from the vector of its segments, prose
            strings and quotes; a payload of another kind is refused.
        ~~|
    | throws :ElementNotSegmentError
        |~~ Every element of a doc is a segment, a string of prose or a
            quote, and another value is refused at the index it stands at;
            a number is made prose by `toString` first,
            `get ["a" ~(5 | toString)] | tag ::doc`.
        ~~|
    | proves :isThePageOfAName
        |~~ The page of a name is a doc. ~~|
        ~(::number/add | doc | type | eq ::doc)
    | proves :splitsIntoSegments
        |~~ A doc of prose, code and prose has three segments. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | segments | count | eq 3)
    | proves :buildsFromSegments
        |~~ Segments under `::doc` build the doc. ~~|
        ~(["a" ~(add 1)] | tag ::doc | eq (|~~a~(add 1)~~|))
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string payload is refused. ~~|
        ~(::doc"x" !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::doc/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::string])
    | proves :ElementNotSegmentError/refusesANumberAmongTheSegments
        |~~ A number among the segments is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~(["a" 1] | tag ::doc !| get [~(type) /index] | eq [::doc/ElementNotSegmentError 1])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:doc/content
  |~~ Answers the text of the doc, a string: its prose with each quote
      written where it stands, `|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | content` answering
      `" a ~(add 1) b "`, and a link or a snippet with its tags,
      `::link~(::vec | doc)`. The subject is a doc, so the page of a
      name is read by `doc` first, `::vec/join | doc | content`; a name
      declared without a page has none, `doc` answering null, which
      `content` refuses as it refuses a string. The parts of the doc in
      their order `segments` answers, and its links `links`.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::doc/segments | doc)
      ::link~(::doc/links | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The text of the doc, its quotes written where they stand. ~~|
        ::string
    | throws :SubjectNotDocError
        |~~ `content` reads a doc: the page of a name is read by `doc`
            first, `::vec/join | doc | content`, and a name declared
            without a page has none, `doc` answering null.
        ~~|
    | proves :writesEachQuoteWhereItStands
        |~~ The content writes each quote where it stands in the prose. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | content | eq " a ~(add 1) b ")
    | proves :writesALinkWithItsTags
        |~~ A link is written with its tags. ~~|
        ~(|~~ see ::link~(::vec | doc) ~~| | content | eq " see ::link~(::vec | doc) ")
    | proves :readsThePageOfAVerb
        |~~ The content of a verb's page is its text. ~~|
        ~(::vec/join | doc | content | contains "separator")
    | proves :SubjectNotDocError/refusesTheNullOfANameWithoutAPage
        |~~ A name declared without a page has no content to read. ~~|
        ~(:x 1 | ::x | doc | content
          !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::doc/content/SubjectNotDocError ::null])
    | proves :SubjectNotDocError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no doc. ~~|
        ~("x" | content !| type | eq ::doc/content/SubjectNotDocError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:doc/segments
  |~~ Answers the parts of the doc in their order, a vector: each run of
      prose a string, and each span that reads as code a quote, bare or
      under the tags of its role, a link under `::link` and a snippet
      under `::snippet`, `|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | segments` answering
      `[" a " ~(add 1) " b "]`, each tag laid over the quote through its
      label as a tagged literal lays it, so a tag that takes no quote for
      its payload refuses the doc, `::number~(…)` answering
      `::number/PayloadKindNotTakenError`. A doc of prose alone answers a
      vector of one string.
      A span that does not read as code stays prose, so a quote left
      broken in a page is no link and no snippet. The segments are the
      payload the doc was built from, so `segments | tag ::doc` answers
      the doc again, and `content` writes them as one text. The subject
      is a doc, the page of a name read by `doc` first.
      ::link~(::doc/content | doc) ::link~(::doc/links | doc)
      ::link~(::doc/snippets | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The prose and the quotes of the doc, in their order. ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :SubjectNotDocError
        |~~ `segments` reads a doc, not its text: the page of a name is
            read by `doc` first, `::vec | doc | segments`.
        ~~|
    | proves :alternatesProseAndCode
        |~~ Prose and code alternate in the segments. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | segments | eq [" a " ~(add 1) " b "])
    | proves :keepsTheTagsOfALink
        |~~ A link stands among the segments under its tag. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ::link~(1) ~~| | segments | get /1 | type | eq ::link)
    | proves :answersOneStringForProseAlone
        |~~ A doc of prose alone is one segment of prose. ~~|
        ~(|~~ only prose ~~| | segments | eq [" only prose "])
    | proves :keepsABrokenQuoteInProse
        |~~ A span that does not read as code stays prose. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ~(1 | ) b ~~| | segments | count | eq 1)
    | proves :buildsTheDocAgainUnderItsTag
        |~~ The segments under `::doc` are the doc again. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | :d it | d | segments | tag ::doc | eq d)
    | proves :refusesARoleWhosePayloadIsNoQuote
        |~~ A quote under a tag that takes no quote refuses the doc. ~~|
        ~(|~~ see ::number~(~(x)) ~~| !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::quote])
    | proves :SubjectNotDocError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no doc. ~~|
        ~("text" | doc/segments !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::doc/segments/SubjectNotDocError ::string])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:doc/links
  |~~ Answers the links of the doc in their order, a vector: each a quote
      under a stack of tags that holds `::link`, `::link~(::vec | doc)`
      or a host's `::Host::link~(…)`, the query a reader follows to
      another page. A doc without links answers the empty vector, and a
      snippet is no link. A link opens to what its query answers, so
      `::vec | doc | links | map ~open` reads every page the page of vectors
      names, in one query, and `parse` spells its query. The subject is
      a doc, the page of a name read by `doc` first.
      ::link~(::quote/open | doc) ::link~(::doc/snippets | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The links of the doc, each a query that answers another page.
        ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :SubjectNotDocError
        |~~ `links` reads a doc, not its text: the page of a name is read
            by `doc` first, `::vec | doc | links`.
        ~~|
    | proves :findsTheLinkInProse
        |~~ A link inside prose is found. ~~|
        ~(|~~ see ::link~(::vec | manifest) ~~| | links | count | eq 1)
    | proves :findsALinkUnderAHostTag
        |~~ A link under a host's tag is a link. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ::Host::link~(1) b ~~| | links | count | eq 1)
    | proves :leavesTheSnippetsOut
        |~~ A doc whose only quote is a snippet has no link. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ::snippet~(1) b ~~| | links | eq [])
    | proves :runsWhereItLeads
        |~~ A link runs the query it holds. ~~|
        ~(|~~ ::link~(::vec | manifest) ~~| | links | first
          | :l it | (null | apply l | has ::vec/count))
    | proves :SubjectNotDocError/refusesItsText
        |~~ The text of a page is no doc. ~~|
        ~(::vec | doc | content | links
          !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::doc/links/SubjectNotDocError ::string])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:doc/snippets
  |~~ Answers the snippets of the doc in their order, a vector: each a
      quote under a stack of tags that holds `::snippet`, a query a
      reader runs to see what it shows, `::snippet~([2 1] | sort)`. A
      doc without snippets answers the empty vector, and a link is no
      snippet. A snippet opens to the answer it shows, so
      `snippets | map ~open` runs every example of a page. The subject is
      a doc, the page of a name read by `doc` first.
      ::link~(::quote/open | doc) ::link~(::doc/links | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The snippets of the doc, each a query a reader runs. ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :SubjectNotDocError
        |~~ `snippets` reads a doc, not its text: the page of a name is
            read by `doc` first, `::any/manifest | doc | snippets`.
        ~~|
    | proves :findsTheSnippetInProse
        |~~ A snippet inside prose is found. ~~|
        ~(|~~ run ::snippet~([2 1] | sort) ~~| | snippets | count | eq 1)
    | proves :leavesTheLinksOut
        |~~ A doc whose only quote is a link has no snippet. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ::link~(1) b ~~| | snippets | eq [])
    | proves :opensToWhatItShows
        |~~ A snippet opens to what it shows. ~~|
        ~(|~~ ::snippet~([2 1] | sort) ~~| | snippets | map ~open | eq [[1 2]])
    | proves :SubjectNotDocError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no doc. ~~|
        ~("text" | snippets !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::doc/snippets/SubjectNotDocError ::string])
    | impl ::builtin{}

